*.log

# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Output of the go coverage tool, specifically when used with LiteIDE
*.out

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
# go.work  #I want to include this at least for now

# Scratch notes and analysis, not part of the project
analysis.md
.scratch/

# Screen captures written by internal/trace under -tags debugtrace.
# Anchored with a leading slash: an unanchored "trace/" matches at any depth and would
# swallow internal/trace/, the source package that writes these.
/trace/

# Screen captures written by the scripted demo under -tags demoplay. Anchored for the same
# reason as /trace/ above. These are a *view* of the game rather than art it loads, and a run
# regenerates all of them, so committing them would only ever produce a diff of fifty
# near-identical PNGs.
/demo/

# Tool sheets are committed, and live in docs/sheets/ *(owner's call, 2026-08-23)*.
#
# They used to be ignored, on the argument that a run regenerates all of them and the diff is
# only ever near-identical binaries. What that argument left out is the audience: the sheets are
# how the catalogues get *reviewed*, and requiring a Go toolchain and four commands to see them
# means they are only ever looked at by whoever just changed the thing. Committed, a clone opens
# docs/sheets/index.html and sees every card, ring, worm and hand in the game.
#
# The price is real and accepted: about 1.7 MB and 150 binary files rewritten whenever the sheets
# are regenerated. **Regenerate deliberately rather than reflexively** — a sheet rebuilt in a
# commit that changed nothing about it is pure history weight. tools/glyphsheet already commits
# its picture for the review-diff reason; this is the same trade taken wider.
